  "Number": 17,
  "Question": "If \\(\\theta\\) is an acute angle and \\(\\sin2\\theta=a,\\) then \\(\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\sqrt{a+1}\\)",
    "B": "\\((\\sqrt2-1)a+1\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\sqrt{a+1}-\\sqrt{a^2-a}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\sqrt{a+1}+\\sqrt{a^2-a}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\sqrt{a+1}+a^2-a\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The identity \\(\\sin2\\theta=2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\) gives a product of sine and cosine. Squaring the requested sum reveals exactly that product:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta)^2\n&=\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta\n  +2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=1+\\sin2\\theta\\\\\n&=1+a.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\theta\\) is acute, both \\(\\sin\\theta\\) and \\(\\cos\\theta\\) are positive. Their sum must therefore be the positive square root:\n\\[\n\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta=\\boxed{\\sqrt{a+1}}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
